# Test Evidence Pack: {{APP_NAME}}

Re-runnable tests and evidence for the requirements of **{{APP_NAME}}**. The pack ships beside the app as a zip, not inside it, and runs against the deployed app without changing it. The zip already holds the last run's evidence: open `evidence/traceability-matrix.html` to read it before installing anything.

It has three parts:

| Part | Where | What it's for |
|---|---|---|
| Playwright tests | `tests/` | One test per requirement or user-journey step, tagged with the requirement's id and named by a test id of its own (`TEST-007`). Re-run them at any time, and keep them as the app's regression suite. |
| Traceability matrix | `evidence/traceability-matrix.html` (also `.md`, `.csv`) | For each requirement: where it lives in the app, the tests that cover it, the result, and links to the evidence. |
| HTML report | `evidence/html-report/` | A trace and screenshots for every test, so you can watch a requirement pass or fail without running anything. |

The requirements are in `requirements/requirements.json`. Their ids are the ones the Business Specification and the Implementation Report use, so you can look any result up in either document. Each test also has its own id (`TEST-007`), which the matrix, the full report and the Implementation Report show beside it, so a test the report cites is easy to find here.

## Rerun the tests

After any change to the app, rerun the tests from the results page. Open a terminal in this folder and run:

```
npm start
```

That opens the results in your browser. Click **Rerun tests**: the page shows each test as it finishes, then reloads with the new results. To check one fix, open a requirement and click **Rerun** on it instead: only its tests run, and its new result shows on that row beside the last full run's (marked RERUN), with a link to that rerun's own report. The first rerun on a machine also installs what the tests need (the packages, a Chromium browser, and a `.env` copied from `.env.example`), which takes a few minutes. Press Ctrl+C in the terminal to stop.

If you work with Claude, ask it to "rerun the tests": it runs `npm test` and reads `evidence/summary.json`.

To set it up and run it from the terminal instead: You need Node.js 20.12 or later (LTS recommended). The commands are the same in Terminal, PowerShell and cmd.

```
npm ci
npm run setup
copy .env.example .env      # Windows (cmd)
cp .env.example .env        # macOS / Linux / PowerShell
npm test
```

`npm test` runs every test and then builds the matrix. Open `evidence/traceability-matrix.html` in a browser. It shows the pass rate, then every requirement under its section; open one to read its tests as Given / When / Then scenarios, with the failure and its screenshot for a failed test. Click a screen or tag chip to show only those requirements, and click a test for its trace and screenshots in the full report, which has the same look. To browse the full report with traces, run:

```
npm run report
```

To run only some requirements:

```
npm run test:req -- {{EXAMPLE_ID}}
```

That writes its own report and matrix to `evidence/subset/`, listing only those requirements, and leaves the full run's results in `evidence/` as they were. To run one test, give its id instead: `npm run test:req -- TEST-007`.

On a corporate network, `npm run setup` downloads Chromium through the proxy. If it fails, set `HTTPS_PROXY` first.

## Test another environment

Change `APP_BASE_URL` in `.env` to the app's root URL in that environment, then run `npm test` again. The tests read data but don't change it, unless you set `ALLOW_WRITES=true`. Leave that off on any environment other people use.

## Settings (`.env`)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `APP_BASE_URL` | Root URL of the app. |
| `APP_REVISION` | The app revision the tests run against. The matrix names it, so each result says which build it is about. |
| `APP_USER`, `APP_PASSWORD` | Optional user for the sign-in tests. Tests that need it are skipped, with the reason, when it's blank. |
| `APP_USER_WITHOUT_ROLE`, `APP_PASSWORD_WITHOUT_ROLE` | Optional user without the app's privileged role, for the role-gate tests. |
| `ALLOW_WRITES` | `true` runs tests that create or change data. |
